<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benjamin Franklin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1706">1706</a>–<a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benjamin Franklin was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> printer, writer, scientist and statesman. He founded a library, a fire company and the academy that became the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, and became famous for his experiments with electricity. He helped draft the Declaration of Independence, won the French alliance and negotiated the peace of 1783. A breakaway state beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachians</a> was named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin.jpg" alt="Benjamin Franklin" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph-Siffred Duplessis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Siffrein_Duplessis_-_Benjamin_Franklin_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Benjamin Franklin was born on Milk Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 17 January 1706 and baptised at the Old South Meeting House. His father, Josiah Franklin, born at Ecton in Northamptonshire, was a tallow chandler and soap and candle maker. Benjamin was his fifteenth child and tenth and last son. His mother, Abiah Folger, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantucket">Nantucket</a>, and both parents were pious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a>.</p><p>His father could afford to send him to school for only two years, and he continued to educate himself by reading. At 12 he became an apprentice to his brother James, a printer. When James founded The New-England Courant, Franklin wrote letters in the name of Silence Dogood, a middle-aged widow, and neither the readers nor his brother knew the author. He later left the apprenticeship without permission and so became a fugitive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Franklin became a successful printer and editor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, publishing The Pennsylvania Gazette at 23 and Poor Richard's Almanack under the pseudonym Richard Saunders. In 1736 he created the Union Fire Company, and in 1743 he founded the American Philosophical Society. In 1749 he printed Proposals Relating to the Education of Youth in Pensilvania and was appointed president of the Academy, which opened in 1751.</p><p>His electrical research began in the 1740s. He proposed that vitreous and resinous electricity were one fluid under different pressures, was the first to call them positive and negative, and discovered the principle of conservation of charge. He invented the lightning rod, the Franklin stove and bifocal glasses and never patented them. As an agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he led the repeal of the Stamp Act, and as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he secured French support for the Revolutionary War. He served as President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> from 1785 to 1788.</p><p>Franklin owned at least seven slaves from 1735 and advertised slaves for sale in his newspaper, but by the late 1750s he had begun to argue against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and later became an active abolitionist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Franklin died of a pleuritic attack at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on 17 April 1790, aged 84. About 20,000 people attended his funeral, and the assembly of revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> went into mourning for three days. He left £1,000 each to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and Philadelphia, to gather interest for 200 years. He is the only person to have signed the Declaration of Independence, the Treaty of Paris with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the Constitution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benjamin Franklin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Benjamin Franklin</b> pointed to the union of the Six Nations when advocating colonial union, and the Albany Congress of 1754 met partly to renew the alliance with them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Beccaria wrote in Milan, and <b>Benjamin Franklin</b>, printer, scientist of electricity and diplomat, became the American Enlightenment’s most famous figure in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Congress postponed the debate for three weeks and, on 11 June, appointed the Committee of Five: John Adams, <b>Benjamin Franklin</b>, Thomas Jefferson, Robert R. Livingston and Roger Sherman.</blockquote><a href="/work/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The title phrase is said to come from <b>Benjamin Franklin</b>, who was very popular in France during his stay as the Continental Congress's representative from 1776 to 1785 and reportedly answered questions about the American war with &quot;Ça ira, ça ira&quot; in his imperfect French.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Benjamin Franklin</b> and the Swedish envoy Gustaf Philip Creutz signed a treaty of amity and commerce in Paris.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Founded by <b>Benjamin Franklin</b> and Dr Thomas Bond, the Pennsylvania Hospital admitted its first patients in Philadelphia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-11">11 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.36,-71.06&amp;year=1760" aria-label="Boston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2827 657 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Benjamin Franklin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3277 927h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3277 927h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.36,-71.06&amp;year=1760">What was here in 1760? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/william-franklin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-franklin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">William Franklin</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 1730–1813</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the first Place, Gentlemen, you are to consider, that a great Empire, like a great Cake, is most easily diminished at the Edges.&#8221;</p><footer>Rules by Which a Great Empire May Be Reduced to a Small One, 1773 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Rules_By_Which_A_Great_Empire_May_Be_Reduced_To_A_Small_One" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…when you assemble a number of men, to have the advantage of their joint wisdom, you inevitably assemble with those men all their prejudices, their passions, their errors of opinion, their local interests, and their selfish views.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the close of the Constitutional Convention, 1787 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Disapproving_and_accepting_the_Constitution" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;So convenient a thing is it to be a reasonable creature, since it enables one to find or make a reason for everything one has a mind to do.&#8221;</p><footer>Autobiography &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20203/pg20203-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He gave me, accordingly, three great puffy rolls. I was surpris'd at the quantity, but took it, and, having no room in my pockets, walk'd off with a roll under each arm, and eating the other.&#8221;</p><footer>Autobiography, on arriving in Philadelphia in 1723 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20203/pg20203-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I conceived the bold and arduous project of arriving at moral perfection. I wish'd to live without committing any fault at any time…&#8221;</p><footer>Autobiography, part 2 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20203/pg20203-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Human felicity is produced not so much by great pieces of good fortune that seldom happen, as by little advantages that occur every day.&#8221;</p><footer>Autobiography &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20203/pg20203-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
